@feather812002 Good idea, but also we can start by getting insights into some business by only providing hight level description and we might learn a lot about what comes out. And then you can cherry pick what you want and possibly change PRD and ask the cofounder engine to start from updated PRD. So I would vote for such a feature.
It is not clear why many versions are output and what is the reason (maybe ask the engine to provide a reasonable explanation why a new version was introduced. Is it part of redesign request and when enough is enough.
It would be good on individual level of files we can lock it and not change or maybe lock some parts of the text or code and let the engine work around it.

Does anyone want to collaborate with me to thoroughly analyze this project and understand its organization? We could use debugging tools and proxy the messages sent to the LLMs to examine what's being passed and generated. The goal would be to understand the process and sequence of events, and identify where we might introduce "connectors" for different LLMs. Additionally, we could explore integrating other boilerplate platforms and frameworks and find ways to generate pages from components and templates.

[Telosys](https://www.telosys.org/) is a lightweight, pragmatic code generator that uses a Java CLI and the [Velocity Template Language (VTL)](https://velocity.apache.org/engine/1.7/vtl-reference.html), based on a DSL modeling language. Once we uncover the entity relationships, we could prompt the LLM to produce output in Telosys DSL syntax, which would give us a starting point.

Telosys has a concept of template bundles, which provide a way to organize boilerplate code in a desired structure. This might be helpful for setting up the framework we need.

https://github.com/telosys-templates
